SET content = E'## TCP/IP Protocol Suite - Four-Layer Model, IPv4 vs. IPv6 & Packet Transmission\n\nThe TCP/IP model is the practical implementation framework for internet communication, developed by DARPA in the 1970s and formally standardized through IETF RFCs. Unlike the theoretical 7-layer OSI model, TCP/IP collapses communication into four functional layers, each mapping to one or more OSI layers.\n\n## The Four-Layer TCP/IP Model\n\n| TCP/IP Layer | OSI Equivalent | Key Protocols | PDU |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Application | Layers 5, 6, 7 | HTTP, HTTPS, FTP, DNS, SMTP, SSH, HL7 | Data |\n| Transport | Layer 4 | TCP (RFC 793), UDP (RFC 768) | Segment / Datagram |\n| Internet | Layer 3 | IP (RFC 791), ICMP, OSPF, BGP | Packet |\n| Network Access | Layers 1, 2 | Ethernet 802.3, Wi-Fi 802.11, ARP | Frame / Bits |\n\n## IPv4 vs. IPv6 Header Architecture\n\nThe shift from IPv4 to IPv6 was driven by IPv4 address exhaustion (32-bit address space provides approximately 4.3 billion unique addresses) and the need for a simpler, more scalable packet format. IPv6 uses 128-bit addresses, providing 3.4 x 10^38 unique addresses.\n\n| Field | IPv4 (RFC 791) | IPv6 (RFC 8200) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Header Size | 20-60 bytes (variable) | 40 bytes (fixed) |\n| Address Length | 32 bits (4 octets) | 128 bits (16 octets) |\n| Address Format | Dotted-decimal (192.168.1.1) | Colon-hex (2001:db8::1) |\n| Checksum | Included in header | Removed (handled by transport) |\n| Fragmentation | Routers can fragment | Only source host fragments |\n| NAT Required | Yes (address scarcity) | No (vast address space) |\n| Auto-config | DHCP required | SLAAC built-in (RFC 4862) |\n\n## Stateful vs. Stateless Packet Transmission\n\nThe two primary Transport layer protocols represent the fundamental trade-off between reliability and performance.\n\n### TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) - Stateful / Connection-Oriented\n\n- Three-way handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) before data transfer\n- Sequence numbers track every byte; out-of-order segments are reordered\n- Acknowledgements confirm receipt; unacknowledged segments are retransmitted\n- Flow control via sliding window prevents receiver buffer overflow\n- Used by: HTTP/S, FTP, SMTP, SSH, HL7 - any protocol requiring guaranteed delivery\n\n### UDP (User Datagram Protocol) - Stateless / Connectionless\n\n- No handshake; datagrams sent immediately without session establishment\n- No sequence numbers, no acknowledgements, no retransmission\n- Receiver gets data in arrival order, not transmission order\n- Minimal header overhead (8 bytes vs. TCP''s 20+ bytes)\n- Used by: DNS lookups, DHCP, VoIP, video streaming, online gaming\n\n## Healthcare IT Context\n\nIn hospital networks, protocol selection directly impacts clinical workflows:\n- **HL7 messaging** uses TCP (port 2575) because lost ADT messages could cause patient safety events\n- **Medical device telemetry** may use UDP for real-time vital sign streaming where latency matters more than perfect delivery\n- **DICOM imaging** uses TCP (port 104) because incomplete radiology images are clinically useless\n- **DNS resolution** for EHR login uses UDP (port 53) for speed; zone transfers between DNS servers use TCP\n\n## CompTIA A+ Core 1 Exam Focus Areas\n\nCompTIA A+ Core 1 (220-1201) Section 2.1 Protocol Suites tests your ability to:\n\n- Distinguish between the 4-layer TCP/IP model and the 7-layer OSI model and map protocols to their correct layers\n- Identify when TCP vs. UDP is used for a given application scenario\n- Explain the TCP three-way handshake and its role in establishing a reliable session\n- Understand IPv4 vs. IPv6 addressing, including CIDR notation and SLAAC auto-configuration\n- Know that NAT is required with IPv4 but not with IPv6 due to its enormous address space\n\n## References & Citations\n\n- [RFC 791: Internet Protocol](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc791) - IPv4 specification\n- [RFC 793: Transmission Control Protocol](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc793) - TCP specification\n- [RFC 8200: Internet Protocol Version 6](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8200) - IPv6 specification\n- [CompTIA A+ Core 1 (220-1201) Exam Objectives](https://www.comptia.org/certifications/a) - Section 2.1 Protocol Suites'
